-10-20"},"hashtags":["elmeknimrbridge","khartoum"],"bestLight":"sunset","summary":"The El Mek Nimr Bridge is a bridge that links the downtown area of Khartoum, Sudan, with the adjacent city of Khartoum North across the Blue Nile river.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"El Mek Nimr Bridge","photo":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Elmek_Nimir_Bridge.jpg/1280px-Elmek_Nimir_Bridge.jpg","thumb":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Elmek_Nimir_Bridge.jpg/500px-Elmek_Nimir_Bridge.jpg","w":1200,"h":800,"author":"Arwa51o","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elmek_Nimir_Bridge.jpg"}},{"id":"khartoum_omdurman_bridge","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Omdurman Bridge","category":"landmark","tags":["views","photo","sunset"],"location":{"lat":15.61379,"lng":32.49113},"visitMinutes":30,"priceLevel":0,"photoSeed":"khartoum_omdurman_bridge","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":9,"commonsNearby":0,"wikiPageviews":80,"wikidataSitelinks":5,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["omdurmanbridge","khartoum"],"bestLight":"sunset","summary":"The Omdurman Bridge is a steel truss bridge in Sudan on the road connecting Khartoum on the White Nile to Omdurman.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Omdurman Bridge","photo":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Omdurman%2Cold-bridge.jpg/1280px-Omdurman%2Cold-bridge.jpg","thumb":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Omdurman%2Cold-bridge.jpg/500px-Omdurman%2Cold-bridge.jpg","w":1200,"h":697,"author":"Bertramz","license":"CC BY 3.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omdurman,old-bridge.jpg"}},{"id":"khartoum_st_matthew_s_cathedral","areaId":"khartoum","name":"St. Matthew's Cathedral","category":"monument","tags":["history","photo"],"location":{"lat":15.61049,"lng":32.53391},"visitMinutes":45,"priceLevel":0,"photoSeed":"khartoum_st_matthew_s_cathedral","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":0,"commonsNearby":3,"wikiPageviews":124,"wikidataSitelinks":10,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["stmatthewscathedral","khartoum"],"hours":{"week":[{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"}],"note":"Typical hours — check the official site"},"summary":"St. Matthew's Cathedral is a Catholic church in Khartoum, which is the capital of Sudan. The cathedral is located on the banks of the Blue Nile, next to the Mac Nimir Bridge. It is the seat of the Archbishop of Khartoum, though the bishop is currently displaced due to the ongoing Sudanese civil war.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"St. Matthew's Cathedral, Khartoum","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/df/St._Matthew%27s_Catholic_Cathedral_%28Khartoum%29_001.jpg","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/df/St._Matthew%27s_Catholic_Cathedral_%28Khartoum%29_001.jpg","w":1200,"h":1600,"author":"Petr Adam Dohnálek","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0 cz","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/cz/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Matthew%27s_Catholic_Cathedral_(Khartoum)_001.jpg"}},{"id":"khartoum_tuti_bridge","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Tuti Bridge","category":"landmark","tags":["views","photo","sunset"],"location":{"lat":15.60834,"lng":32.51239},"visitMinutes":30,"priceLevel":0,"photoSeed":"khartoum_tuti_bridge","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":1,"commonsNearby":1,"wikiPageviews":83,"wikidataSitelinks":9,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["tutibridge","khartoum"],"bestLight":"sunset","summary":"The Tuti Bridge is a modern suspension bridge linking between Tuti Island and the City of Khartoum in Sudan that was completed in February 2008. In the past the only approach to Tuti Island was via ferries but thanks to the Tuti-Khartoum Bridge, easy access to the island is possible.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Tuti Bridge","photo":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Khartoum%2C_Sudan_%2832229241710%29.jpg/1280px-Khartoum%2C_Sudan_%2832229241710%29.jpg","thumb":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Khartoum%2C_Sudan_%2832229241710%29.jpg/500px-Khartoum%2C_Sudan_%2832229241710%29.jpg","w":1200,"h":800,"author":"Christopher Michel from San Francisco, USA","license":"CC BY 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khartoum,_Sudan_(32229241710).jpg"}},{"id":"khartoum_al_nilin_mosque","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Al-Nilin Mosque","category":"monument","tags":["history","photo"],"location":{"lat":15.61764,"lng":32.48769},"visitMinutes":45,"priceLevel":0,"photoSeed":"khartoum_al_nilin_mosque","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":0,"commonsNearby":0,"wikiPageviews":108,"wikidataSitelinks":11,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["alnilinmosque","khartoum"],"hours":{"week":[{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"}],"note":"Typical hours — check the official site"},"summary":"The Al-Nilin Mosque is a mosque in Omdurman, Sudan. It is located on the western banks of the Nile river, just opposite to the confluence of the White and Blue Nile. It was established in the 1970s during the Nimeiry era of Sudan, and since then remains one of the fine architectural religious venues in the country.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Al-Nilin Mosque","photo":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Masjed_Alnileen.JPG/1280px-Masjed_Alnileen.JPG","thumb":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Masjed_Alnileen.JPG/500px-Masjed_Alnileen.JPG","w":1200,"h":638,"author":"Asim","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masjed_Alnileen.JPG"}},{"id":"khartoum_al_merrikh_stadium","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Al-Merrikh Stadium","category":"other","tags":["family"],"location":{"lat":15.64128,"lng":32.47156},"visitMinutes":90,"priceLevel":2,"photoSeed":"khartoum_al_merrikh_stadium","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":8,"commonsNearby":2,"wikiPageviews":236,"wikidataSitelinks":14,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["almerrikhstadium","khartoum"],"summary":"Al-Merrikh Stadium, also known as the Red Castle, is a multi-use stadium in Omdurman, Sudan, used mostly for football matches and also sometimes for athletics. Established in 1962. The stadium is used mostly for football games and is considered the home stadium of both Al-Merrikh SC and the Sudan national team.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Al-Merrikh Stadium","photo":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Al-Marekh_Stadium_Omdurman_Sudan_1964_Designed_by_Abdel-Moneim_Mustafa_8.jpg/1280px-Al-Marekh_Stadium_Omdurman_Sudan_1964_Designed_by_Abdel-Moneim_Mustafa_8.jpg","thumb":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Al-Marekh_Stadium_Omdurman_Sudan_1964_Designed_by_Abdel-Moneim_Mustafa_8.jpg/500px-Al-Marekh_Stadium_Omdurman_Sudan_1964_Designed_by_Abdel-Moneim_Mustafa_8.jpg","w":1200,"h":675,"author":"Abdel-Moneim Mustafa","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Marekh_Stadium_Omdurman_Sudan_1964_Designed_by_Abdel-Moneim_Mustafa_8.jpg"}},{"id":"khartoum_al_hilal_stadium","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Al-Hilal Stadium","category":"other","tags":["family"],"location":{"lat":15.64722,"lng":32.475},"visitMinutes":90,"priceLevel":2,"photoSeed":"khartoum_al_hilal_stadium","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":0,"commonsNearby":0,"wikiPageviews":484,"wikidataSitelinks":11,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["alhilalstadium","khartoum"],"summary":"The Al-Hilal Stadium, nicknamed The Blue Jewel is a multi-use stadium located in Omdurman, Khartoum State, Sudan. It is mostly used for football matches and is also used for athletics. It is the home of Al-Hilal Club and has a capacity of 25,000.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Al-Hilal Stadium"},{"id":"khartoum_khartoum_stadium","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Khartoum Stadium","category":"other","tags":["family"],"location":{"lat":15.59839,"lng":32.5156},"visitMinutes":90,"priceLevel":2,"photoSeed":"khartoum_khartoum_stadium","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":0,"commonsNearby":0,"wikiPageviews":272,"wikidataSitelinks":13,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["khartoumstadium","khartoum"],"summary":"The Khartoum International Stadium is a multi-purpose stadium in Khartoum, Sudan. It is used mostly for football matches. The stadium has a capacity of 23,000 people. It is also the home stadium of the Sudanese national football team. It was renovated for the 2011 African Nations Championship.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Khartoum Stadium"},{"id":"khartoum_wv_sufi_dancing","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Sufi dancing","category":"landmark","tags":["photo"],"location":{"lat":15.62571,"lng":32.4633},"visitMinutes":60,"priceLevel":1,"photoSeed":"khartoum_wv_sufi_dancing","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsNearby":4,"wikidataSitelinks":0,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["sufidancing","khartoum"],"summary":"Rightly recommended as a must do for visitors to Khartoum. Every Friday (except during Ramadan) tourists and visiting foreign aid workers flock to the Hamed al-Nil tomb with cameras in hand. A very colourful and noisy celebration. It's about 2&nbsp;km south of the Omdurman souq - ask your taxi driver to go to Ghobba al-Hamed al-Nil.","summarySource":"wikivoyage","wikivoyageTitle":"Khartoum","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Friday_afternoon_Dervishes_%288625532149%29.jpg","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Friday_afternoon_Dervishes_%288625532149%29.jpg","w":1200,"h":900,"author":"David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada","license":"CC BY 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friday_afternoon_Dervishes_(8625532149).jpg"}},{"id":"khartoum_wv_souq_arabi","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Souq Arabi","category":"market","tags":["markets","food"],"location":{"lat":15.60234,"lng":32.52453},"visitMinutes":60,"priceLevel":0,"photoSeed":"khartoum_wv_souq_arabi","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsNearby":2,"wikidataSitelinks":0,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["souqarabi","khartoum"],"hours":{"week":[{"open":"08:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"08:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"08:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"08:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"08:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"08:00","close":"18:00"},null],"note":"Closed Sun; Typical hours — check the official site"},"bestLight":"morning","summary":"If you want to see crowds and action, this is the place. The commercial heart of town, Souq Arabi (Arabian Market) can provide everything you need (see \"Buy\" section for more details on shopping options). The market is huge and spread over several squares in the center of Khartoum proper just south of the Great Mosque (Mesjid al-Kabir) and the minibus station. Come here to see Khartoumites go abou","summarySource":"wikivoyage","wikivoyageTitle":"Khartoum","photo":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Old_center_Khartoum.jpg/1280px-Old_center_Khartoum.jpg","thumb":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Old_center_Khartoum.jpg/500px-Old_center_Khartoum.jpg","w":1200,"h":1600,"author":"Mussapedia","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_center_Khartoum.jpg"}},{"id":"khartoum_wv_sudan_presidential_palace_museum","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Sudan Presidential Palace Museum","category":"museum","tags":["museums"],"location":{"lat":15.608,"lng":32.52904},"visitMinutes":120,"priceLevel":2,"photoSeed":"khartoum_wv_sudan_presidential_palace_museum","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsNearby":1,"wikidataSitelinks":0,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["sudanpresidentialpalacemuseum","khartoum"],"hours":{"week":[null,{"open":"10:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"10:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"10:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"10:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"10:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"10:00","close":"18:00"}],"note":"Closed Mon; Typical hours — check the official site"},"summary":"This museum is located in the grounds of the Presidential Palace and is housed in the impressive century old Palace Cathedral. The palace contains many relics and pieces related to Sudan's administrative and modern political history, from paintings of Sir Gordon Pasha to the Presidential cars of more recent rulers.","summarySource":"wikivoyage","wikivoyageTitle":"Khartoum","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/Republican_Palace_Museum_%288625532655%29.jpg","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/Republican_Palace_Museum_%288625532655%29.jpg","w":1200,"h":900,"author":"David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada","license":"CC BY 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Republican_Palace_Museum_(8625532655).jpg"}},{"id":"khartoum_wv_the_khalifa_s_house","areaId":"khartoum","name":"The Khalifa's House","category":"landmark","tags":["photo"],"location":{"lat":15.63901,"lng":32.4885},"visitMinutes":60,"priceLevel":1,"photoSeed":"khartoum_wv_the_khalifa_s_house","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsNearby":1,"wikidataSitelinks":0,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["thekhalifashouse","khartoum"],"summary":"Abdullah al-Taaisha, also known as \"The Khalifa\" succeeded as leader of the Mahdists on the death of the Mahdi. His house is now a museum and is worth a visit if you want to get a flavour of Omdurman's history. Exhibits include various remnants from old battles and some interesting old British newspaper clippings reporting the British efforts in the Sudan.","summarySource":"wikivoyage","wikivoyageTitle":"Khartoum","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Tomb_of_The_Mahdi_%288626640228%29.jpg","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Tomb_of_The_Mahdi_%288626640228%29.jpg","w":1200,"h":900,"author":"David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada","license":"CC BY 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_The_Mahdi_(8626640228).jpg"}},{"id":"khartoum_wv_confluence_of_the_blue_and_white_nile","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Confluence of the Blue and White Nile","category":"landmark","tags":["photo"],"location":{"lat":15.61494,"lng":32.49212},"visitMinutes":60,"priceLevel":1,"photoSeed":"khartoum_wv_confluence_of_the_blue_and_white_nile","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsNearby":0,"wikidataSitelinks":0,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["confluenceoftheblueandwhitenile","khartoum"],"summary":"If you have the stamina, you will reach the confluence of the two branches of the Nile if you continue walking west along Nile Street for about 3–4&nbsp;km from the Presidential Palace. The confluence is called Al-Mogran and it is best seen from the metal bridge (the old one, not the newer concrete one) linking Khartoum and Omdurman or Al-Mogran Family Park. It is said that one can actually see th","summarySource":"wikivoyage","wikivoyageTitle":"Khartoum","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Confluence_of_the_Nile_%288626639594%29.jpg","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Confluence_of_the_Nile_%288626639594%29.jpg","w":1200,"h":900,"author":"David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada","license":"CC BY 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confluence_of_the_Nile_(8626639594).jpg"}},{"id":"khartoum_wv_sudan_ethnographic_museum","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Sudan Ethnographic Museum","category":"museum","tags":["museums"],"location":{"lat":15.60799,"lng":32.53424},"visitMinutes":120,"priceLevel":2,"photoSeed":"khartoum_wv_sudan_ethnographic_museum","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsNearby":0,"wikidataSitelinks":0,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["sudanethnographicmuseum","khartoum"],"hours":{"week":[null,{"open":"10:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"10:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"10:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"10:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"10:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"10:00","close":"18:00"}],"note":"Closed Mon; Typical hours — check the official site"},"summary":"A fascinating little museum on the corner of Al Gamaa street and Mak Nemir Avenue. This museum explores the various cultures and traditions of Sudan's numerous ethnic groups. Displays range from intricate handicrafts to models of traditional homes. This museum is certainly worth a visit, if you have the time.","summarySource":"wikivoyage","wikivoyageTitle":"Khartoum"},{"id":"khartoum_wv_souq_omdurman","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Souq Omdurman","category":"landmark","tags":["photo"],"location":{"lat":15.64918,"lng":32.48243},"visitMinutes":60,"priceLevel":1,"photoSeed":"khartoum_wv_souq_omdurman","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsNearby":0,"wikidataSitelinks":0,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["souqomdurman","khartoum"],"summary":"Said to be one of the largest markets in Africa and you can get handicrafts here. The handicraft street is quite difficult to find - it is towards the northern end of the market, near the gold section (not on the western side, as stated in the Bradt guide). The street is actually a covered lane between two buildings with gates at either end. It's not very busy (in comparison with the rest of the m","summarySource":"wikivoyage","wikivoyageTitle":"Khartoum"},{"id":"khartoum_wv_bombed_al_shifa_pharmaceutical_factory","areaId":"khartoum","name":"Bombed Al-Shifa pharmaceutical factory","category":"landmark","tags":["photo"],"location":{"lat":15.64639,"lng":32.56156},"visitMinutes":60,"priceLevel":1,"photoSeed":"khartoum_wv_bombed_al_shifa_pharmaceutical_factory","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsNearby":0,"wikidataSitelinks":0,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["bombedalshifapharmaceuticalfactory","khartoum"],"summary":"a fascinating place to explore, and well off the Khartoum tourist trail (if Khartoum even has such a thing). Tomahawked in 1998 the remains are an eerie reminder of Sudan's precarious relationship with the West. It's quite easy to reach - get a minibus to Bahri, then get a taxi.  The guards are quite friendly and will let you in for a wander - a contribution would be gratefully received.","summarySource":"wikivoyage","wikivoyageTitle":"Khartoum"}],"sources":["Wikidata (CC0)","Wikimedia Commons (counts; photos credited per file)","Wikipedia (pageviews; summaries CC BY-SA 4.0)","Wikivoyage (listings: hours, prices, descriptions; CC BY-SA 4.0)","OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL): opening hours, fees, websites"]}